ggml-cpu: pause a thread's previous threadpool when it starts a graph on another one

Two threadpools driven one after the other by the same thread (a speculative draft model and its target) busy-wait
against each other on shared cores: after each graph, the idle pool's workers spin for 1024*128*poll relax rounds
while the other pool needs those cores. Stock llama.cpp hits this with every draft model (the target pool spins while
the draft's throwaway pool computes), and so does a draft with its own -td/-Cd pools.
When a thread starts a graph on a pool other than its last one, it now pauses that last pool. Only a live pool
(a registry under the global critical section) with no graph in flight (a busy flag set at kickoff, cleared when
ggml_graph_compute returns, after the final barrier) is paused, so no worker can sleep through a kicked-off graph;
pausing another thread's pool between kickoff and pickup would deadlock. A paused pool resumes at its next kickoff.
Desktop, 3 strict cores, MiniCPM5-2B Q4_0 + DSpark Q4_0, greedy (same text in every run): draft with its own pools
(-td 2) 16.7 -> 37.4 tok/s; draft sharing the target's pools (0002) unchanged, 37.5. On stock llama.cpp (4 cores) only
7.7 -> 10.9-12.7: there the draft still creates and joins throwaway threads for every graph, which 0002 removes.
Review (2026-11-03): no deadlock or use-after-free found (busy clears after the final barrier; pause and kickoff
share the pool mutex; lock order is critical section -> pool mutex only; free unregisters before destroying). Side
effect: resuming a paused pool re-applies its priority and affinity to the calling thread, two syscalls per switch.
Upstream PR: not opened yet.

